연구에서 얻은 동기 (interactive activity recognition 과 mobile computing) 

When I was an undergraduate research assistant, I developed a fully automatic table tennis scoring system using commodity wearable devices and smartphones. In this project, the score was automatically calculated by analyzing multimodal signals, including accelerometer signals and sound signals, and passing messages through the Wifi-Direct, a wireless network technology of smartphones. This system was a novel approach in that it is multi-user activity recognition without additional servers or devices to receive data from both players. I was fascinated by a new value created by interaction of mobile devices, and it gave me a strong motivation to do in-depth research in ubiquitous computing and mobile devices. Strong backgrounds of electronic engineering and mobile computing of the University of Texas at Austin is the main reason why I applied for Electrical and Computing Engineering Ph.D program. (132)

관심있는 연구 분야와 교수

In my doctoral program, I would like to study cooperative mobile computing and online learning on mobile devices for human well-being, such as multi-user activity recognition or context-aware computing. From my academic and professional experiences, due to privacy issues, I have experienced many limitations to collect and utilize data in central servers. Moreover, even if deep learning has its potential, there have been many limitations for trained models to be sustainable in a continuous data stream. My research goal is to build privacy-free cooperative mobile computing systems for creating a new value, such as automatic table tennis scoring system, and to develop robust deep learning architecture in a continuous data stream for human well-being. In the University of Texas at Austin, Dr. Edison Thomaz and Dr. Christine Jullie are the most suitable professors for me. Dr. Edison Thomaz’s research on active learning with wearable is the best research fit for me in the aspect of continual machine learning. Dr. Christine Jullie’s research on federating learning is also the best research for me in the aspect of cooperative computing on mobile devices. (181)

연구 및 실무 경험 (학부생 연구)
I have prepared academically and professionally for graduate school. As an undergraduate researcher under the guidance of Dr. Ahyoung Choi, I researched multi-user activity recognition based on wearable devices and developed real-time monitoring systems for human signals: electrocardiogram, photoplethysmogram, sleep and respiration data. 
My research on multi-user activity recognition helped me to develop problem solving skill with understanding of wireless network and message passing architecture. A big challenge was how the system could be designed without additional devices and Internet network. I proposed synchronized message passing architecture on mobile devices with Wifi-Direct. This architecture improved the usability of the automatic table tennis scoring system in case of no Internet environment and no additional servers to collect data and calculate scores. I published multiple papers and applied for a patent related to this research.   
My project about real-time systems for human signals improved my basic engineering skills related to building backend system: TCP servers, web servers and databases. For example, I was involved in the project of unobtrusive tele-monitoring system for sleep and respiration. I developed TCP servers and databases for 1 kHz data collected from sensors and placed queues for data buffering. The web servers took and displayed sensor data from buffers in real time. I kept working on this project even if graduated my university and got a job. This shows my passion for my research with my advisor, Ahyoung Choi. The project was published in a journal, IEEE Access. (241)

연구 및 실무 경험 (삼성전자)
At Samsung Electronics, I was involved in multiple projects concerning machine learning and various types of big data. For example, in the first year, I analyzed time series data and developed a reinforcement learning model to manage emulation resources for chip design. As there were few references on the project, the project seemed to be unsuccessful. However, I didn't give up and looked up references from other domains, such as networking. Fortunately, I came up with reinforcement learning and developed a Double Deep Q Network model for the project. In recognition of the company's cost reduction and publishing paper at the top conference, I received the Best Paper Award from my company. Nowadays, I develop a prioritization algorithm based on clustering terabytes of log data from chip simulation.
I have also built system architecture for machine learning pipelines in a continuous data stream. Before my project, my team struggled with high latency of machine learning model and tedious tasks for machine learning development and deployment. To solve these challenges, I proposed an efficient and scalable system. The system is composed of in-memory machine learning models and automated machine learning pipelines. This architecture decreased inference time from 40 seconds to 1 second on average and reduced the number of tedious machine learning development and deployment steps. (215)

